<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volga</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Volga River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Volga is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, flowing through central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. It was a major artery of trade linking the Baltic and northern forests with the Caspian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the Islamic world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> controlled its lower course, with its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atil">Atil</a> near the river's mouth, and profited greatly from the commerce passing along it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volga.jpg" alt="Volga" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexxx1979 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yaroslavl._Volga_River._Cathedral_of_the_Dormition_P5212700_2200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
